Q1. Partitioned STUDENT table (15 marks)

Step 1: Create the table with range partitioning on Marks
 CREATE DATABASE college;
 USE college;

 CREATE TABLE STUDENT (
    Roll_No    INT NOT NULL,
    Name       VARCHAR(30),
    Department VARCHAR(20),
    Marks      INT NOT NULL,
    PRIMARY KEY (Roll_No, Marks)
 )
 PARTITION BY RANGE (Marks) (
    PARTITION p0 VALUES LESS THAN (40),
    PARTITION p1 VALUES LESS THAN (60),
    PARTITION p2 VALUES LESS THAN (80),
    PARTITION p3 VALUES LESS THAN MAXVALUE
 );

Note: in MySQL the partitioning column (Marks) must be part of the primary key, so the key is (Roll_No,
Marks).

Step 2: INSERT
 INSERT INTO STUDENT VALUES
 (101,'Amit','IT',35),
 (102,'Sneha','CS',55),
 (103,'Rahul','IT',72),
 (104,'Priya','CS',88),
 (105,'Kiran','IT',45),
 (106,'Neha','CS',91);

 SELECT * FROM STUDENT;
Roll_No                     Name                        Department                 Marks
101                         Amit                        IT                         35
102                         Sneha                       CS                         55
105                         Kiran                       IT                         45
103                         Rahul                       IT                         72
104                         Priya                       CS                         88
106                         Neha                        CS                         91

Rows come out grouped by partition (p0, p1, p2, p3), not in insertion order.

Step 3: Display records from each partition separately
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p0);
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p1);
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p2);
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p3);
Partition                               Marks range                             Records
p0                                      below 40                                101 Amit (35)
p1                                      40 to 59                                102 Sneha (55), 105 Kiran (45)
p2                                      60 to 79                                103 Rahul (72)
p3                                      80 and above                            104 Priya (88), 106 Neha (91)

Step 4: UPDATE
   UPDATE STUDENT SET Marks = 65 WHERE Roll_No = 101;

   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p0);                -- Empty set
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p2);

Amit's marks changed from 35 to 65, so MySQL moved the row from partition p0 to p2 automatically. p0 is now
empty and p2 has 101 Amit (65) and 103 Rahul (72).

Step 5: DELETE
   DELETE FROM STUDENT WHERE Roll_No = 106;

   SELECT * FROM STUDENT PARTITION (p3);

Only 104 Priya (88) is left in p3.

Final data
   SELECT * FROM STUDENT;
Roll_No                       Name                          Department                    Marks
102                           Sneha                         CS                            55
105                           Kiran                         IT                            45
101                           Amit                          IT                            65
103                           Rahul                         IT                            72
104                           Priya                         CS                            88

Working of partition-wise data storage
-    Partitioning splits one large logical table into smaller physical parts called partitions. The table is still used
    as one table in queries.
-    On INSERT, the database applies the partition rule to the Marks value and stores the row in the matching
    partition. For example, 72 is less than 80, so it goes to p2.
-    On UPDATE, if the partitioning column changes, the row is moved to the correct partition.
-    On SELECT with a condition on Marks, the optimizer reads only the needed partitions. This is called
    partition pruning, and it makes queries faster.
-    Each partition can be stored, backed up, or dropped separately (for example ALTER TABLE STUDENT DROP
    PARTITION p0;). This makes large tables easy to manage.
-    Types: RANGE (ranges of values, used above), LIST (fixed value lists), HASH (hash of a column, spreads rows
    evenly). Hash example: PARTITION BY HASH(Roll_No) PARTITIONS 4;

-    To see pruning: EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM STUDENT WHERE Marks = 72; shows only p2 in the partitions
    column.

Q2. Distributed transactions, COMMIT, ROLLBACK and recovery (15 marks)

Distributed transaction
A distributed transaction is a single transaction that updates data on two or more database servers (nodes)
connected by a network. Even though the data is on different sites, the whole transaction must behave as one
unit: either all sites save the changes, or none do. This follows the ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency,
Isolation, Durability).

Bank money-transfer example
Rahul has an account in Bank A at Site 1 (Pune) and Sneha has an account in Bank B at Site 2 (Mumbai). Rahul
transfers Rs. 5,000 to Sneha.

Step                                    Site                              Action
1                                       Site 1 (Bank A)                   Debit Rs. 5,000 from Rahul
2                                       Site 2 (Bank B)                   Credit Rs. 5,000 to Sneha
3                                       Both sites                        If both steps succeed: COMMIT. If any
                                                                         step fails: ROLLBACK

   -- Site 1 (Bank A)
   UPDATE accounts_A SET balance = balance - 5000 WHERE acc_no = 1001;

   -- Site 2 (Bank B)
   UPDATE accounts_B SET balance = balance + 5000 WHERE acc_no = 2002;

   COMMIT;      -- both updates become permanent
   -- if the credit fails:
   ROLLBACK;    -- the debit is also undone

Money must never be debited at one site without being credited at the other. This is why the transaction is
treated as one atomic unit.

Complete coding example (MySQL)
Both banks are shown as two tables here so the script runs on one MySQL server. In a real distributed system
each table sits on a different site.

Step 1: Create tables and insert data

   CREATE DATABASE bankdb;
   USE bankdb;

   CREATE TABLE accounts_A (acc_no INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(30), balance INT);
   CREATE TABLE accounts_B (acc_no INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(30), balance INT);

   INSERT INTO accounts_A VALUES (1001,'Rahul',20000);
   INSERT INTO accounts_B VALUES (2002,'Sneha',10000);

Step 2: Successful transfer of Rs. 5,000 with COMMIT

   START TRANSACTION;
   UPDATE accounts_A SET balance = balance - 5000 WHERE acc_no = 1001;
   UPDATE accounts_B SET balance = balance + 5000 WHERE acc_no = 2002;
   COMMIT;

   SELECT * FROM accounts_A;            -- 1001 Rahul 15000
   SELECT * FROM accounts_B;            -- 2002 Sneha 15000

Step 3: Failed transfer with ROLLBACK (the credit step fails, so the debit is undone)

   START TRANSACTION;
   UPDATE accounts_A SET balance = balance - 5000 WHERE acc_no = 1001;
   -- wrong account number, so no row is credited (0 rows affected)
   UPDATE accounts_B SET balance = balance + 5000 WHERE acc_no = 9999;
   ROLLBACK;     -- the debit is cancelled

   SELECT * FROM accounts_A;            -- still 1001 Rahul 15000

Step 4: Distributed transaction with two-phase commit (MySQL XA)

   XA START 'transfer1';
   UPDATE accounts_A SET balance = balance - 5000 WHERE acc_no = 1001;
   XA END 'transfer1';
   XA PREPARE 'transfer1';   -- Phase 1: site votes YES
   XA COMMIT 'transfer1';    -- Phase 2: commit

   -- if any site votes NO or fails, use instead of XA COMMIT:
   -- XA ROLLBACK 'transfer1';

   -- the same XA steps run on Site 2 for accounts_B (credit 5000)

Output summary: after Step 2 Rahul has 15000 and Sneha has 15000 (total unchanged at 30000). After Step 3
nothing changes because ROLLBACK restored the old values.

Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol
-    Phase 1, Prepare (voting): the coordinator asks every site, "Can you commit?" Each site writes to its log and
    replies YES or NO.
-    Phase 2, Commit or Abort: if all sites reply YES, the coordinator sends COMMIT to all. If any site replies NO
    or does not reply, the coordinator sends ROLLBACK to all.

Purpose of COMMIT and ROLLBACK
COMMIT                                                   ROLLBACK
Saves all changes of the transaction permanently.        Cancels all changes made since the transaction began.
Makes the changes visible to other users.                Restores the database to its previous consistent state.
Ends the transaction successfully.                       Used when an error or failure occurs.
Example: transfer completed at both banks.               Example: credit to Sneha failed, so the debit from Rahul is
                                                        cancelled.

Why transaction recovery is required
-    Failures can happen at any time: system crash, power cut, network failure between sites, disk failure, or a
    site going down in the middle of a transaction.
-    Without recovery, the database could be left half-updated (money debited but not credited), which breaks
    Atomicity and Consistency.

-   Recovery uses the transaction log. Committed transactions are redone (REDO) so Durability is kept, and
   uncommitted transactions are undone (UNDO) so no partial changes remain.
-   In a distributed system, recovery also makes sure all sites reach the same decision (all commit or all abort),
   even if one site fails after voting.
-   So recovery keeps the data correct, consistent and reliable after any failure.

Conclusion
Distributed transactions keep data consistent across sites using 2PC. COMMIT makes changes permanent,
ROLLBACK cancels them, and recovery guarantees the database returns to a correct state after a failure.

