Q7. Explain the Star Schema with a suitable example of a college or
supermarket database. Identify the fact table and any three dimension
tables.
A star schema is a database design used in data warehouses. It has one central fact table
that stores measurable business data (numbers), connected directly to dimension tables
that store descriptive data. The dimension tables are denormalized (kept as single tables),
and when drawn, the design looks like a star: the fact table in the middle and dimension
tables around it.

Example: Supermarket sales data warehouse
               Dim_Product
                    |
Dim_Customer ---- Fact_Sales ---- Dim_Date
Fact table (one):

-   Fact_Sales(Sale_ID, Product_ID, Customer_ID, Date_ID, Quantity, Amount) - stores
   the measures (Quantity sold, Amount) and the foreign keys that link to each
   dimension. One row per sale.

Dimension tables (three):

-   Dim_Product(Product_ID, Product_Name, Category, Brand, Price) - describes each
   product. Category and Brand are kept in the same table (denormalized).
-   Dim_Customer(Customer_ID, Name, City, State, Phone) - describes each customer.
   City and State are kept in the same table.
-   Dim_Date(Date_ID, Day, Month, Quarter, Year) - describes the date of the sale, so
   sales can be analysed month-wise or year-wise.

Sample rows
Fact_Sales
Sale_ID | Product_ID | Customer_ID | Date_ID | Quantity | Amount
  1    |    P101    |    C501     | D20260101|    2     | 1500.00
  2    |    P102    |    C502     | D20260101|    1     | 450.00
  3    |    P101    |    C503     | D20260102|    3     | 2250.00

Dim_Product                            Dim_Customer

P101 | Rice 5kg | Grocery              C501 | Amit | Pune
P102 | Soap     | Personal             C502 | Neha | Mumbai
P103 | Milk 1L | Dairy                 C503 | Rahul | Nashik

Dim_Date
D20260101 | 01 | Jan | Q1 | 2026
D20260102 | 02 | Jan | Q1 | 2026
Why it is called a star and how it is used

-   The fact table is at the centre and each dimension table joins directly to it, so the
   diagram looks like a star.
-   Because dimensions are denormalized, a query needs only one join per dimension, so
   reporting queries are fast and simple.
-   Example query: total sales category-wise for January 2026 = join Fact_Sales with
   Dim_Product and Dim_Date, group by Category.
-   Star schemas are the standard design for OLAP and data warehouse reporting (sales
   analysis, college admissions analysis, attendance analysis).

Conclusion: in a star schema the fact table holds the numbers (measures) and the
dimension tables hold the descriptions, giving a simple, fast design for analysis.

Q8. Write minimum 10 complex queries on MongoDB collections using
various keywords and operators.
Setup: database StudentDB, collection students (same data as Q3):
db.students.insertMany([
{ roll_no: 1, name: "Aayan", department: "IT",   marks: 85, address:
{ city: "Pune" } },
{ roll_no: 2, name: "Rahul", department: "CS",   marks: 78, address:
{ city: "Mumbai" } },
{ roll_no: 3, name: "Sneha", department: "IT",   marks: 92, address:
{ city: "Nashik" } },
{ roll_no: 4, name: "Amit", department: "ENTC", marks: 70, address:
{ city: "Satara" } },
{ roll_no: 5, name: "Neha", department: "IT",    marks: 88, address:
{ city: "Nagpur" }, hobbies: ["music","chess"] },
{ roll_no: 6, name: "Karan", department: "CS",   marks: 65, address:
{ city: "Nagpur" }, phone: "9876543210" }
])
Query 1: Comparison operator ($gt) with AND condition - IT students with marks
above 80
db.students.find({ department: "IT", marks: { $gt: 80 } })
Output: Aayan (85), Sneha (92), Neha (88) -> 3 documents

Query 2: Logical operator $or - CS students OR students with marks below 70
db.students.find({ $or: [ { department: "CS" }, { marks: { $lt: 70 } } ]
})
Output: Rahul (CS, 78), Karan (CS, 65) -> 2 documents

Query 3: $in operator with projection - only name and marks of IT and CS
students, without _id
db.students.find({ department: { $in: ["IT", "CS"] } }, { _id: 0, name:
1, marks: 1 })
Output: Aayan, Rahul, Sneha, Neha, Karan (name and marks only) -> 5 documents

Query 4: Range condition ($gte, $lte) with sorting - marks between 70 and 90,
highest first
db.students.find({ marks: { $gte: 70, $lte: 90 } }).sort({ marks: -1 })
Output: Neha (88), Aayan (85), Rahul (78), Amit (70) -> 4 documents

Query 5: skip() and limit() - the student with the 2nd highest marks
db.students.find().sort({ marks: -1 }).skip(1).limit(1)
Output: Neha (88) -> 1 document

Query 6: Nested field (dot notation) - students whose address city is Pune
db.students.find({ "address.city": "Pune" })
Output: Aayan -> 1 document

Query 7: Regular expression ($regex) - names starting with "A"
db.students.find({ name: { $regex: "^A" } })
Output: Aayan, Amit -> 2 documents

Query 8: $exists operator - documents that have a hobbies field
db.students.find({ hobbies: { $exists: true } })
Output: Neha -> 1 document

Query 9: $nor operator - students who are NOT from IT and do NOT have marks
below 60
db.students.find({ $nor: [ { department: "IT" }, { marks: { $lt:
60 } } ] })
Output: Rahul, Amit, Karan -> 3 documents

Query 10: countDocuments() with a condition - number of IT students
db.students.countDocuments({ department: "IT" })
Output: 3

Query 11 (bonus): Update operator $inc on many documents - give 5 grace marks to
all IT students
db.students.updateMany({ department: "IT" }, { $inc: { marks: 5 } })
Output: { matchedCount: 3, modifiedCount: 3 }

Query 12 (bonus): Aggregation $group with $avg - average marks per department
db.students.aggregate([ { $group: { _id: "$department", avgMarks:
{ $avg: "$marks" } } } ])
Output: IT -> 88.33, CS -> 71.5, ENTC -> 70

Keywords and operators covered

-   Comparison: $gt, $lt, $gte, $lte
-   Logical: $or, $nor (and $and, $not work the same way)
-   Array / element: $in, $exists
-   String: $regex
-   Cursor methods: sort(), skip(), limit(), countDocuments()
-   Update: $inc (also $set, $mul); Aggregation: $group with $avg, $sum

Note: outputs were checked by hand against the sample data, not run on a live MongoDB
server. Run them once in mongosh or Compass before the exam.


